{"name":"Dark Princess Pink","hex":"#d9308a","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having an absolute or (more often) relative lack of light.","Extinguished."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A complete or (more often) partial absence of light.","Ignorance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dark"},{"term":"princess","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female member of a royal family other than a queen, especially a daughter or granddaughter of a monarch. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A woman or girl who excels in a given field or class."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/princess"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dark Princess","blocks":[{"text":"Dark Princess, written by sociologist W. E. B. Du Bois in 1928, and entered the United States public domain in 2024, is one of five historical novels. Considered one of his favorite works, the novel explores themes of double consciousness, racial solidarity, and romance as a form of double meaning. Through its blending of romance, fiction, and realism, Du Bois examines historical experiences in a way that the non-fiction genre does not allow. The lives of the characters in the novel, every success, failure, or heartbreak, were created to correlate with real historical events. The novel received mixed reactions from both early and modern-day readers. It was often recognized for how it distinguished itself from Du Bois's early works."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Dark_Princess_%281928%2C_DuBois%29_1st_Edition_Dust_Jacket.jpg/330px-Dark_Princess_%281928%2C_DuBois%29_1st_Edition_Dust_Jacket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Princess","wikidata":"Q5223362"},{"title":"Princess","blocks":[{"text":"Princess is a title used by a female member of a regnant monarch's family or by a female ruler of a principality. The male equivalent is a prince. Most often, the term has been used for the consort of a prince, or for the daughter of a monarch. A crown princess can be the heir apparent to the throne or the spouse of the heir apparent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Sigrid_Eriksdotter_Vasa.jpg/330px-Sigrid_Eriksdotter_Vasa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess","wikidata":"Q863048"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Dark_Princess_%281928%2C_DuBois%29_1st_Edition_Dust_Jacket.jpg/500px-Dark_Princess_%281928%2C_DuBois%29_1st_Edition_Dust_Jacket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Dark_Princess_%281928%2C_DuBois%29_1st_Edition_Dust_Jacket.jpg/1280px-Dark_Princess_%281928%2C_DuBois%29_1st_Edition_Dust_Jacket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia